1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,976
This is MATH 239, video 2. The topic is multisets and multiplicity vectors.

2
00:00:07,426 --> 00:00:12,034
After this video, you can write a multiset as a multiplicity vector.

3
00:00:12,484 --> 00:00:16,665
You can prove Theorem 1.9, which counts multisets.

4
00:00:17,115 --> 00:00:20,465
You can tell lists, subsets, and multisets apart.

5
00:00:20,915 --> 00:00:28,189
And you can solve tutorial 1, questions 1 and 3 part a, and practice problem P1-3.

6
00:00:28,639 --> 00:00:42,570
Section 1.1.4 starts with a bag of marbles. The marbles have three colours: red, green, and blue. There are very many marbles of each colour. Marbles of the same colour look the same.

7
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:45,772
Take out a handful of 11 marbles.

8
00:00:46,222 --> 00:00:51,321
The order in which the marbles come out does not matter. So sort them by colour.

9
00:00:51,771 --> 00:00:53,669
There are 4 red marbles.

10
00:00:54,119 --> 00:00:56,082
There are 5 green marbles.

11
00:00:56,532 --> 00:00:58,281
There are 2 blue marbles.

12
00:00:58,731 --> 00:01:03,958
So the outcome is the triple 4, 5, 2. The three numbers add to 11.

13
00:01:04,408 --> 00:01:11,085
The outcome is the number of marbles of each colour. The question is: how many outcomes are possible?

14
00:01:11,535 --> 00:01:17,701
Before we count, compare four kinds of choices. Choose 2 things from the set 1, 2, 3.

15
00:01:18,151 --> 00:01:23,548
If the order matters and repeats are allowed, we get all the ordered pairs i, j.

16
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:29,737
The grid has 3 rows and 3 columns. So there are 3 times 3, which is 9.

17
00:01:30,187 --> 00:01:36,800
If the order matters but repeats are not allowed, remove the pairs on the diagonal. They are red.

18
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:41,815
This leaves 3 times 2, which is 6, partial lists of length 2.

19
00:01:42,265 --> 00:01:50,521
If the order does not matter, then 1, 2 is the same choice as 2, 1. Keep only the pairs with i less than j.

20
00:01:50,971 --> 00:01:54,790
Without repeats, these are the 3 subsets of size 2.

21
00:01:55,240 --> 00:02:05,331
The fourth kind: the order does not matter, but repeats are allowed. Keep the pairs with i less than or equal to j. So the diagonal comes back.

22
00:02:05,781 --> 00:02:11,626
There are 6. These are the multisets of size 2. The marbles are this fourth kind.

23
00:02:12,076 --> 00:02:17,153
Definition 1.8. Let n be at least 0, and t at least 1.

24
00:02:17,603 --> 00:02:26,969
A multiset of size n with elements of t types is a sequence of nonnegative integers m 1 to m t, whose sum is n.

25
00:02:27,419 --> 00:02:32,027
Here is the handful again. Put the marbles of each type in a column.

26
00:02:32,477 --> 00:02:36,487
Type 1 is red, type 2 is green, and type 3 is blue.

27
00:02:36,937 --> 00:02:40,180
m i is the number of elements of type i.

28
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:47,201
So the multiset R R R R, G G G G G, B B is the sequence 4, 5, 2.

29
00:02:47,651 --> 00:02:51,512
Tutorial 1 calls this sequence the multiplicity vector.

30
00:02:51,962 --> 00:02:59,130
The definition uses only the numbers. This is correct, because the elements of one type are all the same.

31
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:05,020
Start with small cases. Take size n equals 2, and t equals 3 types.

32
00:03:05,470 --> 00:03:08,713
List the vectors with sum 2. There are 6 of them.

33
00:03:09,163 --> 00:03:16,565
Do the same for other small n and t. The table shows the number of multisets of size n with t types.

34
00:03:17,015 --> 00:03:19,853
With 1 type there is only one multiset.

35
00:03:20,303 --> 00:03:24,953
With 2 types, m 1 can be 0 to n, so there are n plus 1.

36
00:03:25,403 --> 00:03:28,817
Each column contains numbers from Pascal's triangle.

37
00:03:29,267 --> 00:03:35,581
In the column for t equals 3, the numbers are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, 21.

38
00:03:36,031 --> 00:03:41,578
These are n plus 2 choose 2. Theorem 1.9 gives the general pattern.

39
00:03:42,028 --> 00:03:50,967
Theorem 1.9: the number of multisets of size n with t types is n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1.

40
00:03:51,417 --> 00:04:02,894
The proof asks what this number counts. By Theorem 1.5, it counts the subsets of size t minus 1 of a set with n plus t minus 1 elements.

41
00:04:03,344 --> 00:04:15,013
So write a row of n plus t minus 1 circles. The notes use n equals 11 and t equals 3. 11 plus 3 minus 1 is 13 circles.

42
00:04:15,463 --> 00:04:23,101
Choose t minus 1 of the positions, here 2, and cross them out. The notes cross out positions 5 and 11.

43
00:04:23,551 --> 00:04:28,031
The crosses cut the other 11 circles into t segments, here 3.

44
00:04:28,481 --> 00:04:33,665
Let m i be the length of segment i. Here the lengths are 4, 5, and 2.

45
00:04:34,115 --> 00:04:41,987
Their sum is 4 plus 5 plus 2, which is 11. So this is a multiset of size 11 with 3 types.

46
00:04:42,437 --> 00:04:48,218
The proof also goes in the other direction. Start with a multiset m 1 to m t.

47
00:04:48,668 --> 00:04:56,199
Write m 1 circles, then a cross, then m 2 circles, then a cross, and so on. End with m t circles.

48
00:04:56,649 --> 00:04:59,123
Take the multiset 3, 0, 8.

49
00:04:59,573 --> 00:05:04,587
Write 3 circles, a cross, 0 circles, a cross, and 8 circles.

50
00:05:05,037 --> 00:05:11,714
That is 3 plus 0 plus 8, which is 11 circles, and 2 crosses: 13 positions.

51
00:05:12,164 --> 00:05:14,809
The crosses are at positions 4 and 5.

52
00:05:15,259 --> 00:05:20,081
They are next to each other. So segment 2 is empty, and m 2 is 0.

53
00:05:20,531 --> 00:05:24,541
A segment can be empty. So a type can have zero elements.

54
00:05:24,991 --> 00:05:30,602
Here are both directions together, for the small case n equals 2 and t equals 3.

55
00:05:31,052 --> 00:05:33,313
On the left are the 6 multisets.

56
00:05:33,763 --> 00:05:41,806
Each multiset gives a row of 2 plus 3 minus 1, which is 4, symbols: 2 circles and 2 crosses.

57
00:05:42,256 --> 00:05:46,693
Each row gives back its multiset. The arrows go both ways.

58
00:05:47,143 --> 00:05:55,122
Each row is fixed by the positions of its 2 crosses. These positions are a 2 element subset of the set 1 to 4.

59
00:05:55,572 --> 00:06:05,001
Each multiset matches exactly one subset, and each subset matches exactly one multiset. This is a one to one correspondence.

60
00:06:05,451 --> 00:06:11,019
So the numbers are equal: 6 multisets, and 4 choose 2, which is 6, subsets.

61
00:06:11,469 --> 00:06:19,363
In general, a multiset of size n with t types matches a row of n circles and t minus 1 crosses.

62
00:06:19,813 --> 00:06:22,629
The row has n plus t minus 1 positions.

63
00:06:23,079 --> 00:06:29,052
In this drawing, n is 9 and t is 4. So there are 9 circles and 3 crosses.

64
00:06:29,502 --> 00:06:38,995
The row is fixed by the positions of the crosses. They form a subset of size t minus 1 of the set 1 to n plus t minus 1.

65
00:06:39,445 --> 00:06:45,653
The two constructions undo each other. So this is a one to one correspondence, a bijection.

66
00:06:46,103 --> 00:06:55,255
By Theorem 1.5, there are n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1 multisets. This proves Theorem 1.9.

67
00:06:55,705 --> 00:07:00,847
Now answer the marble question. n equals 11, and t equals 3.

68
00:07:01,297 --> 00:07:12,795
So there are 11 plus 3 minus 1 choose 3 minus 1 outcomes. That is 13 choose 2, which is 13 times 12 over 2, or 78.

69
00:07:13,245 --> 00:07:19,944
We can also choose the positions of the n circles. That gives n plus t minus 1 choose n.

70
00:07:20,394 --> 00:07:28,373
It is the same number, by the symmetry of Pascal's triangle. Tutorials often call this method stars and bars.

71
00:07:28,823 --> 00:07:33,601
Now tutorial 1, question 1. A is the set 1, 2, 3, 4.

72
00:07:34,051 --> 00:07:44,462
List all the 2 element subsets, and all the 2 element multisets with elements from A. Check that the numbers are correct. Pause the video and try it.

73
00:07:44,912 --> 00:07:49,840
Write each choice as a pair i, j. The row is i and the column is j.

74
00:07:50,290 --> 00:07:56,455
The subsets of size 2 are the pairs with i less than j. They are green. There are 6.

75
00:07:56,905 --> 00:08:02,132
Theorem 1.5 gives 4 choose 2, which is 6. The count matches.

76
00:08:02,582 --> 00:08:12,609
A multiset can repeat an element. So add the pairs with i equal to j: 1 1, 2 2, 3 3, and 4 4. They are purple.

77
00:08:13,059 --> 00:08:15,747
Now there are 6 plus 4, which is 10.

78
00:08:16,197 --> 00:08:22,874
Check with Theorem 1.9. The size is n equals 2. There are t equals 4 types.

79
00:08:23,324 --> 00:08:32,284
So the number is 2 plus 4 minus 1 choose 4 minus 1. That is 5 choose 3, which is 10. The list is complete.

80
00:08:32,734 --> 00:08:44,595
Part b. Write each subset and each multiset as its multiplicity vector m 1, m 2, m 3, m 4. Here m i is the number of copies of i.

81
00:08:45,045 --> 00:08:50,549
The question also asks which properties the entries have. Pause the video and try it.

82
00:08:50,999 --> 00:08:58,637
The subset 1, 2 has one copy of 1 and one copy of 2. So its vector is 1, 1, 0, 0.

83
00:08:59,087 --> 00:09:02,692
The green column shows the vectors of all 6 subsets.

84
00:09:03,142 --> 00:09:11,953
The multisets are on the right. The multiset 1, 1 has two copies of 1. So its vector is 2, 0, 0, 0.

85
00:09:12,403 --> 00:09:15,048
The purple rows have a repeated element.

86
00:09:15,498 --> 00:09:22,026
The properties: each entry is a nonnegative integer, and the entries add up to k, here 2.

87
00:09:22,476 --> 00:09:28,492
For a set, each element occurs at most once. So each entry of a set is 0 or 1.

88
00:09:28,942 --> 00:09:36,665
Tutorial 1, question 3 part a. A k element multiset is chosen from the set 1 to n plus 1.

89
00:09:37,115 --> 00:09:43,792
Explain why the number of such multisets is n plus k choose k. Pause the video and try it.

90
00:09:44,242 --> 00:09:50,877
Be careful with the letters. Here the size is k, and the number of types is t equals n plus 1.

91
00:09:51,327 --> 00:09:56,767
So we need k circles, and t minus 1 crosses. t minus 1 is n.

92
00:09:57,217 --> 00:10:02,166
An example: n equals 3, so there are 4 types, and k equals 4.

93
00:10:02,616 --> 00:10:10,787
The multiset 2, 1, 0, 1 is this row: 2 circles, a cross, 1 circle, a cross, a cross, and 1 circle.

94
00:10:11,237 --> 00:10:16,357
That is 4 circles and 3 crosses. 3 plus 4 is 7 positions.

95
00:10:16,807 --> 00:10:19,943
The crosses are at positions 3, 5, and 6.

96
00:10:20,393 --> 00:10:30,419
In general, a row has n plus k positions. It is fixed by the positions of the k circles. So there are n plus k choose k multisets.

97
00:10:30,869 --> 00:10:40,811
This also equals n plus k choose n, the positions of the crosses. Theorem 1.9 with n plus 1 types gives the same result.

98
00:10:41,261 --> 00:10:46,893
Practice problem P1-3. Let t be at least 2, and n at least 1.

99
00:10:47,343 --> 00:10:53,999
How many multisets of size n with t types have m 1 equal to 1? Pause the video and try it.

100
00:10:54,449 --> 00:10:58,374
Start with an example: n equals 4 and t equals 3.

101
00:10:58,824 --> 00:11:05,331
The first entry is fixed at 1. So the other two entries add to 4 minus 1, which is 3.

102
00:11:05,781 --> 00:11:07,893
There are 4 such multisets.

103
00:11:08,343 --> 00:11:17,537
Delete the first entry. What is left, m 2 to m t, is a multiset of size n minus 1, with t minus 1 types.

104
00:11:17,987 --> 00:11:23,278
To go back, put the 1 in front again. So this is a one to one correspondence.

105
00:11:23,728 --> 00:11:29,381
Now apply Theorem 1.9 with size n minus 1 and t minus 1 types.

106
00:11:29,831 --> 00:11:44,381
The top is n minus 1, plus t minus 1, minus 1. The bottom is t minus 1, minus 1. So the number is n plus t minus 3 choose t minus 2. This is the posted answer.

107
00:11:44,831 --> 00:11:52,767
Check with the example: n equals 4 and t equals 3 give 4 choose 1, which is 4. This matches the list.

108
00:11:53,217 --> 00:12:03,499
Here is an added example of the same type. How many outcomes of the 11 marbles have at least one marble of each colour? Pause the video and try it.

109
00:12:03,949 --> 00:12:08,707
First put one marble of each colour aside. That uses 3 marbles.

110
00:12:09,157 --> 00:12:14,853
Let m i prime be m i minus 1. Each m i prime is at least 0.

111
00:12:15,303 --> 00:12:20,337
Their sum is 11 minus 3, which is 8. These are the 8 free marbles.

112
00:12:20,787 --> 00:12:28,659
So the outcomes match the multisets of size 8 with 3 types. Adding the 3 marbles back undoes the change.

113
00:12:29,109 --> 00:12:41,013
By Theorem 1.9, the number is 8 plus 3 minus 1 choose 3 minus 1. That is 10 choose 2, which is 10 times 9 over 2, or 45.

114
00:12:41,463 --> 00:12:48,503
One more added example. How many outcomes have at most 2 red marbles? Pause the video and try it.

115
00:12:48,953 --> 00:12:56,932
Split by the value of m 1, the number of red marbles: 0, 1, or 2. The three cases are disjoint.

116
00:12:57,382 --> 00:13:03,867
Case 1: m 1 equals 0. Then m 2 plus m 3 is 11, with 2 types.

117
00:13:04,317 --> 00:13:13,384
By Theorem 1.9, there are 11 plus 2 minus 1 choose 2 minus 1. That is 12 choose 1, which is 12.

118
00:13:13,834 --> 00:13:19,807
Case 2: m 1 equals 1. Then m 2 plus m 3 is 10. There are 11.

119
00:13:20,257 --> 00:13:26,295
Case 3: m 1 equals 2. Then m 2 plus m 3 is 9. There are 10.

120
00:13:26,745 --> 00:13:34,361
The cases are disjoint, so OR means add. The answer is 12 plus 11 plus 10, which is 33.

121
00:13:34,811 --> 00:13:39,739
Here is a summary. To choose k things from n types, ask two questions.

122
00:13:40,189 --> 00:13:41,959
First: does the order matter?

123
00:13:42,409 --> 00:13:44,351
Second: can an element repeat?

124
00:13:44,801 --> 00:13:51,990
No repeats, and the order matters: partial lists. There are n factorial over n minus k factorial.

125
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:57,496
No repeats, and no order: subsets of size k. There are n choose k.

126
00:13:57,946 --> 00:14:03,685
With repeats, and the order matters: n choices, k times. That is n to the k.

127
00:14:04,135 --> 00:14:10,172
With repeats, and no order: a multiset. There are n plus k minus 1 choose k.

128
00:14:10,622 --> 00:14:17,854
Be careful: in this table, n is the number of types. Theorem 1.9 uses t for the number of types.

129
00:14:18,304 --> 00:14:27,221
To recap. A multiset of size n with t types is a vector of nonnegative integers m 1 to m t, with sum n.

130
00:14:27,671 --> 00:14:33,346
Theorem 1.9 says there are n plus t minus 1 choose t minus 1 of them.

131
00:14:33,796 --> 00:14:41,519
The proof writes n circles and t minus 1 crosses in a row. The crosses form a subset of the positions.

132
00:14:41,969 --> 00:14:45,297
Two crosses next to each other mean an empty type.

133
00:14:45,747 --> 00:14:55,901
For a condition, fix an entry, as in P1-3. Or put aside the elements of a lower bound. Or split into disjoint cases and add.

134
00:14:56,351 --> 00:15:03,839
The next video makes the idea of a one to one correspondence exact, with bijections and bijective proofs.
